1
00:00:00,102 --> 00:00:01,530
Eighteen thirty-four.

2
00:00:02,331 --> 00:00:09,319
A sixty-nine-year-old German immigrant
walks into his office in New York and
announces that he is leaving the business
that made him a millionaire.

3
00:00:10,117 --> 00:00:11,715
Employees are stunned.

4
00:00:12,513 --> 00:00:15,634
The fur trade is booming, and he controls
most of it.

5
00:00:16,432 --> 00:00:17,782
He sells everything.

6
00:00:18,581 --> 00:00:22,756
The fur posts, the ships, the trading
routes across a continent.

7
00:00:23,555 --> 00:00:26,923
His rivals are puzzled, because it looks
like retirement.

8
00:00:27,721 --> 00:00:30,308
But John Jacob Astor is not retiring.

9
00:00:31,107 --> 00:00:34,892
He is making the smartest bet of his life,
and it is a bet on dirt.

10
00:00:35,690 --> 00:00:40,855
When he dies, he will be the richest man
in America, and the reason will not be
furs.

11
00:00:41,766 --> 00:00:48,220
John Jacob Astor was born on July
seventeenth, seventeen sixty-three, in the
village of Walldorf,

12
00:00:48,316 --> 00:00:49,158
in Germany.

13
00:00:49,958 --> 00:00:52,962
His father was a butcher, and the family
was poor.

14
00:00:53,760 --> 00:00:57,532
There were four sons, and Johann Jakob was
the youngest.

15
00:00:58,331 --> 00:01:01,360
At sixteen, he left home with almost
nothing.

16
00:01:02,159 --> 00:01:07,259
He went to London to join his brother, who
made musical instruments, and he learned
English.

17
00:01:08,057 --> 00:01:10,123
But London did not offer much.

18
00:01:10,922 --> 00:01:16,165
His brothers were already in America, and
letters spoke of a new country with
endless land.

19
00:01:16,964 --> 00:01:23,287
In seventeen eighty-three, as the American
Revolution ended, the twenty-year-old
boarded a ship to Baltimore.

20
00:01:24,086 --> 00:01:26,790
He had a few flutes to sell and little
else.

21
00:01:27,589 --> 00:01:31,998
Then, during the voyage, he met a fellow
German who had worked in the fur trade,

22
00:01:32,198 --> 00:01:35,410
and who told him how much money could be
made in beaver pelts.

23
00:01:36,208 --> 00:01:38,600
The story goes that Astor listened all
night.

24
00:01:39,398 --> 00:01:43,157
By the time the ship arrived, he knew
exactly what he would do.

25
00:01:43,956 --> 00:01:45,709
Furs were more than fashion.

26
00:01:46,508 --> 00:01:50,045
In those years, a good beaver pelt was a
kind of currency.

27
00:01:50,844 --> 00:01:54,407
A hat made from beaver felt was the sign
of a gentleman in London,

28
00:01:54,503 --> 00:01:55,957
Paris, and Vienna.

29
00:01:56,755 --> 00:02:03,014
The trade moved millions of pelts across
the Atlantic, and the men who controlled
the supply controlled a river of money.

30
00:02:03,942 --> 00:02:07,558
In New York, Astor took a job beating furs
for a dealer.

31
00:02:08,358 --> 00:02:14,786
He learned how to grade pelts, how to spot
cheap ones, and how to bargain with the
Native hunters and trappers.

32
00:02:15,585 --> 00:02:19,747
Then, in seventeen eighty-six, he opened
his own small shop.

33
00:02:20,546 --> 00:02:26,726
He walked the woods of upstate New York,
buying furs directly from trappers and
carrying them on his back.

34
00:02:27,525 --> 00:02:33,680
He traveled to Montreal, where the biggest
fur markets were, and made a deal with the
British trading companies.

35
00:02:34,478 --> 00:02:35,776
His business grew.

36
00:02:36,574 --> 00:02:40,268
He sent furs to London, and he sold them
for huge profits.

37
00:02:41,067 --> 00:02:45,411
By the seventeen nineties, he was one of
the biggest fur dealers in the country.

38
00:02:46,210 --> 00:02:50,841
He married Sarah Todd, who brought a small
dowry and a sharp business mind.

39
00:02:51,640 --> 00:02:55,581
She helped him judge furs, and she ran the
shop while he traveled.

40
00:02:56,379 --> 00:02:58,823
By eighteen hundred, he was wealthy.

41
00:02:59,621 --> 00:03:01,766
But he wanted to be bigger than the
British.

42
00:03:02,564 --> 00:03:04,448
He also made a bold choice.

43
00:03:05,246 --> 00:03:12,391
When Britain and America signed a treaty
in seventeen ninety-four, Astor saw that
the border forts would open to American
traders.

44
00:03:13,189 --> 00:03:21,049
He quickly sent agents into the Great
Lakes and the West, and built
relationships with Native trappers who had
traded with the British for generations.

45
00:03:21,966 --> 00:03:25,517
In eighteen-oh-eight, Astor founded the
American Fur Company.

46
00:03:26,317 --> 00:03:32,537
His plan was audacious: control every step
of the trade, from the trapper in the
forest to the buyer in China,

47
00:03:32,749 --> 00:03:35,154
where sea otter pelts sold for fortunes.

48
00:03:35,953 --> 00:03:39,737
He decided to build a trading post at the
mouth of the Columbia River,

49
00:03:39,911 --> 00:03:42,589
on the Pacific coast, and call it Astoria.

50
00:03:43,387 --> 00:03:48,097
His ships would carry goods around Cape
Horn to the Pacific, take furs to China,

51
00:03:48,283 --> 00:03:51,287
and return with silk, tea, and porcelain.

52
00:03:52,085 --> 00:03:53,618
But the plan was risky.

53
00:03:54,416 --> 00:03:59,008
One ship, the Tonquin, was destroyed in a
fight near Vancouver Island.

54
00:03:59,807 --> 00:04:03,631
A land expedition crossed mountains,
starving and lost.

55
00:04:04,429 --> 00:04:09,490
And then, in eighteen twelve, the war
between Britain and America arrived.

56
00:04:10,289 --> 00:04:15,883
A British warship sailed toward Astoria,
and the local managers, who were Scottish
and Canadian,

57
00:04:16,122 --> 00:04:19,581
sold the fort to a British company for far
less than it was worth.

58
00:04:20,380 --> 00:04:23,748
The loss hurt, but the legend of Astoria
did not die.

59
00:04:24,546 --> 00:04:30,584
Years later, the Oregon Territory would
become part of the United States, and
settlers followed his trail.

60
00:04:31,382 --> 00:04:35,415
The tiny town of Astoria still stands at
the mouth of the Columbia River,

61
00:04:35,666 --> 00:04:38,813
one of the oldest American settlements on
the Pacific coast.

62
00:04:39,611 --> 00:04:42,394
Astor lost about four hundred thousand
dollars.

63
00:04:43,192 --> 00:04:45,844
He was furious, but he did not give up.

64
00:04:46,758 --> 00:04:51,936
After the war, Astor lobbied Congress to
ban foreign traders from American
territory.

65
00:04:52,737 --> 00:04:56,600
Congress agreed, and overnight his British
rivals were shut out.

66
00:04:57,398 --> 00:05:00,272
The American Fur Company grew into a
monopoly.

67
00:05:01,070 --> 00:05:02,485
He used every tool.

68
00:05:03,284 --> 00:05:10,311
Agents offered whiskey to trappers,
advanced credit to keep hunters in debt,
and drove smaller competitors out of
business.

69
00:05:11,109 --> 00:05:15,363
His company controlled most of the fur
trade across the Great Lakes and the West.

70
00:05:16,161 --> 00:05:18,670
But Astor saw something that others
missed.

71
00:05:19,469 --> 00:05:21,509
Beaver hats were going out of style.

72
00:05:22,307 --> 00:05:28,253
In Europe, fashion was turning to silk,
and the supply of beavers in America was
already shrinking.

73
00:05:29,052 --> 00:05:32,954
So in eighteen thirty-four, he sold the
American Fur Company.

74
00:05:33,752 --> 00:05:36,066
The buyers thought they had won a prize.

75
00:05:36,864 --> 00:05:41,821
Astor knew they were buying a sinking
ship, and within a decade the fur trade
collapsed.

76
00:05:42,620 --> 00:05:44,022
His critics hated him.

77
00:05:44,820 --> 00:05:52,134
Editors accused him of cheating Native
hunters, of using alcohol to lower prices,
and of crushing smaller traders.

78
00:05:52,932 --> 00:05:55,701
Astor answered that he was only following
the market.

79
00:05:56,622 --> 00:05:59,496
With his money, Astor turned to another
business.

80
00:06:00,296 --> 00:06:05,188
In the eighteen-hundreds, New York was a
small city at the bottom of Manhattan
island.

81
00:06:05,986 --> 00:06:10,969
Astor looked at the maps and saw that it
would grow north, because it had nowhere
else to go.

82
00:06:11,767 --> 00:06:16,347
He began buying land, farms, and empty
lots on the edge of the city.

83
00:06:17,145 --> 00:06:18,899
He did not build on most of it.

84
00:06:19,697 --> 00:06:25,748
He simply leased it, usually for twenty-
one years, so that tenants would build the
houses and pay him rent.

85
00:06:26,546 --> 00:06:28,729
When the city grew, rents soared.

86
00:06:29,528 --> 00:06:34,589
When a panic crushed the value of land,
Astor bought even more from desperate
sellers.

87
00:06:35,387 --> 00:06:38,808
When the tenants' leases ended, the
buildings reverted to him.

88
00:06:39,606 --> 00:06:44,016
He also built something to remind New York
who he was: the Astor House,

89
00:06:44,189 --> 00:06:47,974
a grand hotel on Broadway, opened in
eighteen thirty-six.

90
00:06:48,773 --> 00:06:53,873
It was one of the first luxury hotels in
America, with running water and a marble
lobby.

91
00:06:54,671 --> 00:06:56,282
It was the perfect machine.

92
00:06:57,080 --> 00:07:02,610
The land made money while he slept, and
every immigrant who arrived in New York
became a customer.

93
00:07:03,534 --> 00:07:06,043
Astor lived modestly in his old age.

94
00:07:06,844 --> 00:07:09,535
He was heavy, stubborn, and lonely.

95
00:07:10,333 --> 00:07:16,358
He kept a small circle of friends,
including the writer Washington Irving,
and he worked from morning to night.

96
00:07:17,156 --> 00:07:23,441
When the Panic of eighteen thirty-seven
struck, he foreclosed on mortgages and
picked up property for pennies.

97
00:07:24,239 --> 00:07:27,204
He also loaned money to farmers who could
not repay.

98
00:07:28,002 --> 00:07:31,462
In the end, he owned thousands of lots
across Manhattan.

99
00:07:32,260 --> 00:07:36,540
He suffered a stroke in his eighties and
lost his ability to manage his business.

100
00:07:37,338 --> 00:07:42,959
He died on March twenty-ninth, eighteen
forty-eight, with a fortune of about
twenty million dollars,

101
00:07:43,159 --> 00:07:45,798
making him the richest man in the United
States.

102
00:07:46,596 --> 00:07:50,707
He left four hundred thousand dollars to
found a public library in New York,

103
00:07:50,841 --> 00:07:52,100
the Astor Library.

104
00:07:52,898 --> 00:07:55,876
It later became part of the New York
Public Library.

105
00:07:56,674 --> 00:08:02,712
And for generations, his heirs, the
Astors, remained among the wealthiest
families on earth.

106
00:08:03,510 --> 00:08:07,490
His son William Backhouse Astor inherited
the empire and doubled it.

107
00:08:08,289 --> 00:08:13,753
William's descendants built mansions on
Fifth Avenue, and one of them founded the
Waldorf Hotel,

108
00:08:13,927 --> 00:08:17,985
which later joined with the Astoria Hotel
to become the Waldorf Astoria.

109
00:08:18,784 --> 00:08:24,131
In nineteen twelve, his great-grandson
John Jacob Astor the Fourth died on the
Titanic,

110
00:08:24,278 --> 00:08:26,071
the richest passenger on the ship.

111
00:08:26,870 --> 00:08:30,928
Over time, the family became a symbol of
American aristocracy.

112
00:08:31,726 --> 00:08:36,852
But the whole dynasty was built on one
immigrant who arrived with a few flutes
and a good idea.

113
00:08:37,782 --> 00:08:40,356
John Jacob Astor made a fortune twice.

114
00:08:41,157 --> 00:08:43,666
First in furs, and then in land.

115
00:08:44,464 --> 00:08:50,970
He knew when to sell a good business, and
he understood that the best investments
are the ones that grow with the people
around them.

116
00:08:51,769 --> 00:08:53,705
His story has two lessons.

117
00:08:54,503 --> 00:08:56,061
The first is to be early.

118
00:08:56,860 --> 00:09:01,804
Astor bought land when it was cheap and
empty, long before anyone else saw its
value.

119
00:09:02,602 --> 00:09:04,616
The second is to know when to leave.

120
00:09:05,414 --> 00:09:10,488
He sold the fur trade at its peak, and
that single decision created the larger
fortune.

121
00:09:11,287 --> 00:09:14,160
And a third: wealth compounds quietly.

122
00:09:14,959 --> 00:09:18,054
Astor did not live in a palace or chase
publicity.

123
00:09:18,852 --> 00:09:23,926
He simply owned things that grew in value,
and he had the patience to let time do the
work.

124
00:09:24,724 --> 00:09:30,541
If you enjoyed this story, subscribe,
because tomorrow at six, another file
opens.

